WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:03.936
-Our first guest tonight is an
Oscar- and Emmy-nominated actor

00:00:03.936 --> 00:00:05.438
you know him from shows
like "The Office" and

00:00:05.438 --> 00:00:06.772
"The Morning Show," as well as

00:00:06.772 --> 00:00:09.308
the films "The 40 Year
Old Virgin" and "Foxcatcher."

00:00:09.308 --> 00:00:12.078
He stars in
"Minions: The Rise of Gru,"

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.913
which opens
in theaters July 1st.

00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:15.348
Let's take a look.

00:00:15.348 --> 00:00:19.852
-You seriously think a puny
little child can be a villain?

00:00:19.852 --> 00:00:23.590
-Um, yes.
I am pretty despicable.

00:00:23.590 --> 00:00:25.758
You don't want to cross me.

00:00:25.758 --> 00:00:29.829
-Evil is for adults who steal

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:32.531
powerful ancient stones
and wreak havoc...

00:00:32.531 --> 00:00:33.967
-[ Gasps ]

00:00:33.967 --> 00:00:38.571
-...and not for tubby little
punks who should be at school.

00:00:38.571 --> 00:00:42.008
-Please welcome to the show
Steve Carell, everybody.

00:00:42.008 --> 00:00:44.544
[ Cheers and applause ]
♪♪

00:00:55.521 --> 00:00:57.390
Welcome.
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:57.390 --> 00:01:00.492
-I'm so happy to be here.
It's a pleasure.

00:01:00.492 --> 00:01:01.727
-You know, obviously,

00:01:01.727 --> 00:01:03.696
you're this
accomplished comedian,

00:01:03.696 --> 00:01:04.998
accomplished voice actor.

00:01:04.998 --> 00:01:07.533
We first met in 2005
when you hosted "SNL."

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.035
And we wrote a sketch.

00:01:09.035 --> 00:01:13.072
It was a Hurricane Katrina
celebrity benefit,

00:01:13.072 --> 00:01:15.507
and we just assumed,
"We've got Steve Carell.

00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:17.644
He must have a
million celebrity impressions."

00:01:17.644 --> 00:01:18.977
That was not the case.
-Not the case.

00:01:18.977 --> 00:01:21.614
-Yeah. And we literally said,
"It could be anyone."

00:01:21.614 --> 00:01:23.415
-Right.
-And you said, "I have no one."

00:01:23.415 --> 00:01:26.486
-I have -- I don't
do impressions. Terrible.

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:28.053
-And do you remember
what we came up with?

00:01:28.053 --> 00:01:30.756
-Ray Romano.
-Yeah.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:33.025
Now, I will say --
we have a still of it --

00:01:33.025 --> 00:01:35.027
you ended up looking
more like Ray Romano

00:01:35.027 --> 00:01:36.362
than I thought you could.

00:01:36.362 --> 00:01:38.298
There's -- I'm Anderson Cooper,
and there you are.

00:01:38.298 --> 00:01:41.033
That's a very...

00:01:41.033 --> 00:01:43.035
-And I was terrified
because, you know,

00:01:43.035 --> 00:01:44.437
I'd never done an impression,

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.807
and "SNL" is
predicated on impressions.

00:01:47.807 --> 00:01:51.310
So, the first rehearsal,
I just kind of whipped out,

00:01:51.310 --> 00:01:52.311
"Uh, yeah.

00:01:52.311 --> 00:01:53.612
Uh, okay."

00:01:53.612 --> 00:01:54.880
And that was it.
-Yeah.

00:01:54.880 --> 00:01:56.382
-"I just kind of
did that to my voice.

00:01:56.382 --> 00:01:58.618
Uh, Ray Romano. Uh, ha."

00:01:58.618 --> 00:01:59.853
-I think that was
the extent of it, too.

00:01:59.853 --> 00:02:01.253
-That was it.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.253 --> 00:02:03.856
-I think everyone realized, "Oh,
we can't give him dialogue."

00:02:03.856 --> 00:02:06.793
"Uh, yeah. How you doing?"

00:02:07.427 --> 00:02:08.661
-It's better than I remember.

00:02:08.661 --> 00:02:09.863
I will say, it's a lot better
than I remember.

00:02:09.863 --> 00:02:11.230
-Yes.

00:02:11.230 --> 00:02:14.066
-Yeah. Do people -- When you --
I mean, first of all,

00:02:14.066 --> 00:02:17.470
this is a raised level of
difficulty here with this Gru.

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.038
Now you got to do a kid Gru.

00:02:19.038 --> 00:02:20.973
-Oh, so hard.
-So hard?

00:02:20.973 --> 00:02:23.842
-Oh, my gosh.
I'm being facetious.

00:02:23.842 --> 00:02:26.412
-Do people ever --
-It's really not hard.

00:02:26.412 --> 00:02:27.546
-Do people ever say to you,

00:02:27.546 --> 00:02:29.916
"How do you come up with
your funny voices?"

00:02:29.916 --> 00:02:31.851
-Never.

00:02:31.851 --> 00:02:34.720
It just doesn't happen.
I think anyone can do Gru.

00:02:34.720 --> 00:02:36.322
I really --
I shouldn't say that,

00:02:36.322 --> 00:02:37.423
'cause I'll be out of a job.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:38.558
-Because they could
probably get it.

00:02:38.558 --> 00:02:39.959
-Well, I've been
doing this, like --

00:02:39.959 --> 00:02:42.094
all this promotional stuff,
and one of them is, like,

00:02:42.094 --> 00:02:46.165
people calling in
and trying to do the Gru voice.

00:02:46.165 --> 00:02:48.167
And I coach them like,
"It's down here.

00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:49.702
Hey, everybody!"

00:02:49.702 --> 00:02:52.672
And people are quite good at it.

00:02:52.672 --> 00:02:56.843
And Little Gru is just
more earnest and higher.

00:02:56.843 --> 00:02:59.045
-Yeah.
-"He's kind of like this."

00:02:59.045 --> 00:03:00.146
-Were you excited when
they told you

00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:01.647
it was time to do Little Gru?

00:03:01.647 --> 00:03:03.549
Did you think, "Oh, we're going
to switch it up a little bit"?

00:03:03.549 --> 00:03:05.885
-Finally. Yeah.

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.620
No, I thought an origin story
was a good idea,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:10.590
especially since
it's set in the '70s.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:14.227
And just the nostalgia of that,
I think you can

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:17.796
really lean into it,
and I think parents are going to

00:03:17.796 --> 00:03:19.165
really dig it --
grandparents, too.

00:03:19.165 --> 00:03:20.699
-Well, that's the
really important thing about

00:03:20.699 --> 00:03:22.568
when you make
these kids' movies.

00:03:22.568 --> 00:03:24.570
The ones that I think
are beloved are ones

00:03:24.570 --> 00:03:26.439
that adults can also enjoy.

00:03:26.439 --> 00:03:29.175
So when you put anything in
there that they can hold on to,

00:03:29.175 --> 00:03:30.543
because you can't send the kid

00:03:30.543 --> 00:03:32.311
to go see Gru on their own --

00:03:32.311 --> 00:03:33.746
-No, no.
-Yeah.

00:03:33.746 --> 00:03:36.249
-Although, you know,
when my kids were little,

00:03:36.249 --> 00:03:38.184
I used to sit and watch,
you know,

00:03:38.184 --> 00:03:40.686
kids' shows with them,
and there were times that

00:03:40.686 --> 00:03:43.389
they were out of --
They walked out of the room,

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:48.595
and I was still watching,
like, "iCarly,"

00:03:48.595 --> 00:03:51.697
and I'm like,
"What's going to happen?"

00:03:51.697 --> 00:03:55.834
And I enjoyed it.

00:03:55.834 --> 00:03:57.770
-Did you worry
about your children

00:03:57.770 --> 00:03:59.372
that they were so dismissive of

00:03:59.372 --> 00:04:01.907
the outcome of "iCarly,"
her fate?

00:04:01.907 --> 00:04:03.709
-No, I hadn't even
considered that.

00:04:03.709 --> 00:04:06.779
I clearly don't
keep track of my kids.

00:04:06.779 --> 00:04:08.982
-I know you to be
a man of great empathy,

00:04:08.982 --> 00:04:11.049
and I want to ask you this,
because when you do press for

00:04:11.049 --> 00:04:13.285
a movie like this,
you are often with Minions.

00:04:13.285 --> 00:04:15.053
And when I say that --
You know, these are obviously --

00:04:15.053 --> 00:04:16.322
I don't need to tell you,

00:04:16.322 --> 00:04:18.123
these are people in
Minion suits.

00:04:18.123 --> 00:04:20.726
-I've heard.
-Yeah.

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:23.162
And, Steve, a lot of times --
This is a summer film.

00:04:23.162 --> 00:04:24.430
You're out -- It's pretty hot

00:04:24.430 --> 00:04:25.898
when you're doing
"Minions" press.

00:04:25.898 --> 00:04:26.966
-You know this day?
-Yeah.

00:04:26.966 --> 00:04:28.601
-It was really hot.

00:04:28.601 --> 00:04:31.003
And this other day,
it was the same.

00:04:31.003 --> 00:04:32.371
Super-hot.

00:04:32.371 --> 00:04:35.774
-Do you ever -- Can you ever
tell that the human beings

00:04:35.774 --> 00:04:38.077
inside the Minion suits
are getting a little hot?

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:42.014
-Yeah. They start to
sputter a little bit.

00:04:42.014 --> 00:04:48.054
We did this promo
where I did this soccer promo

00:04:48.054 --> 00:04:51.390
with a soccer player from Mexico
named Chicharito.

00:04:51.390 --> 00:04:53.592
And, so,
we did this a little bit,

00:04:53.592 --> 00:04:55.961
and there were Minions
on his team

00:04:55.961 --> 00:04:58.664
and these great, you know,
soccer players on my team.

00:04:58.664 --> 00:05:00.900
And they were going to
have a soccer game.

00:05:00.900 --> 00:05:03.002
And it was in L.A.,

00:05:03.002 --> 00:05:05.771
and it had to have been
95, 100 degrees.

00:05:05.771 --> 00:05:09.408
And these people in
the Minion suits were --

00:05:09.408 --> 00:05:11.977
I mean, they're kicking the ball
and running around.

00:05:11.977 --> 00:05:14.580
And where did the Minions go?

00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:17.449
So, they had wrangled
the Minions around the corner,

00:05:17.449 --> 00:05:19.819
where none of
the kids could see.

00:05:19.819 --> 00:05:23.722
And all I saw, like, were
Minion costumes, sort of, like,

00:05:23.722 --> 00:05:27.993
tipped over and, like,
arms and legs of human beings,

00:05:27.993 --> 00:05:31.597
like, hiding behind bushes,
like, drinking water.

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:34.400
It was really -- It's --
Yes, it's a thankless job.

00:05:34.400 --> 00:05:37.303
-It's a level of professionalism
that you have to appreciate

00:05:37.303 --> 00:05:38.437
when you're inside --
when you're an actor

00:05:38.437 --> 00:05:39.571
inside a Minion suit.

00:05:39.571 --> 00:05:41.440
And you know you can't --
You can't lie down

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.508
in the middle of the field

00:05:42.508 --> 00:05:43.576
because there are
a lot of kids there.

00:05:43.576 --> 00:05:44.877
They don't want to see
a Minion go down.

00:05:44.877 --> 00:05:46.211
-No, no. Exactly.

00:05:46.211 --> 00:05:49.015
No, you have to -- You have to
keep your appearances up.

00:05:49.015 --> 00:05:51.016
-What was your -- I mean, again,

00:05:51.016 --> 00:05:53.118
it's weird when you do
a voice for an animated thing.

00:05:53.118 --> 00:05:55.221
I mean, I'm sure, obviously,
you see drawings in advance,

00:05:55.221 --> 00:05:56.355
but you don't see a
finished product

00:05:56.355 --> 00:05:57.723
until a lot of people do.

00:05:57.723 --> 00:05:59.292
What was it like the first time

00:05:59.292 --> 00:06:01.694
you had the idea of Minions
explained to you?

00:06:01.694 --> 00:06:04.196
-I thought,
"Good luck with that,"

00:06:04.196 --> 00:06:07.065
because, well, they -- You know,
they showed me a picture,

00:06:07.065 --> 00:06:09.668
and I thought,
"Well, sure, they're these

00:06:09.668 --> 00:06:13.272
cylindrical yellow objects
that have --

00:06:13.272 --> 00:06:16.642
either they have goggles,
maybe one eye, maybe two eyes."

00:06:16.642 --> 00:06:19.278
It was explained to me
they don't speak English.

00:06:19.278 --> 00:06:21.013
They speak in a gibberish.

00:06:21.013 --> 00:06:23.248
So you can't really
understand them.

00:06:23.248 --> 00:06:25.818
And they're hilarious.

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:27.786
I went, "Alright.

00:06:27.786 --> 00:06:30.489
Well, good. Good for you guys.

00:06:30.489 --> 00:06:31.991
That's a...

00:06:31.991 --> 00:06:35.027
And it turned out to be
a stroke of genius.

00:06:35.027 --> 00:06:38.830
Like, I saw that first movie.
I saw the finished product.

00:06:38.830 --> 00:06:41.701
And you know, you're in a booth

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.303
and you're doing it
pretty much solitary.

00:06:44.303 --> 00:06:47.039
You don't --
You never see any cuts of it.

00:06:47.039 --> 00:06:48.340
You never -- You don't know what

00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:50.509
the movie's going to
look like until you go see it.

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:52.411
And it was magical.

00:06:52.411 --> 00:06:55.547
-Your early acting --
I did not realize this --

00:06:55.547 --> 00:07:00.385
you did some -- like, some
Revolutionary War re-enacting?

00:07:00.385 --> 00:07:02.021
-Jealous?
[ Laughter ]

00:07:03.756 --> 00:07:05.124
-Now, you took this seriously.

00:07:05.124 --> 00:07:06.225
You learned to play
an instrument.

00:07:06.225 --> 00:07:07.459
-I did, yeah.

00:07:07.459 --> 00:07:10.997
My brother and I joined --
I grew up in a town

00:07:10.997 --> 00:07:13.666
outside of Boston,
and all of these little towns

00:07:13.666 --> 00:07:16.569
had their own minuteman corps.

00:07:16.569 --> 00:07:18.471
And it wasn't really
for re-enacting.

00:07:18.471 --> 00:07:21.907
It's, like, mostly parades, and,
you know, the Fourth of July.

00:07:21.907 --> 00:07:24.242
Back in Massachusetts, we have
something called Patriot's Day.

00:07:24.242 --> 00:07:27.879
And, so, you know,
I wanted to be a part of it,

00:07:27.879 --> 00:07:28.914
and so did my brother.

00:07:28.914 --> 00:07:30.183
So he learned to play the drum,

00:07:30.183 --> 00:07:32.118
and I learned
how to play the fife.

00:07:32.118 --> 00:07:34.319
And, so, we got dressed up
in our little costumes

00:07:34.319 --> 00:07:38.056
and we went out and marched
around, and that was kind of it.

00:07:38.056 --> 00:07:43.262
Later on, I had acquired more of
an interest in history,

00:07:43.262 --> 00:07:46.632
so I joined the
British Tenth Regiment of Foot,

00:07:46.632 --> 00:07:48.834
which was much more serious.

00:07:48.834 --> 00:07:50.670
And that's what I was --
like in high school.

00:07:50.670 --> 00:07:54.073
And, like, they would cast
the buttons from the same molds

00:07:54.073 --> 00:07:56.842
that the original buttons
were cast back then.

00:07:56.842 --> 00:07:59.011
And so I kind of geeked out
and got into it.

00:07:59.011 --> 00:08:02.381
-And did you have to have a
certain level of fife playing?

00:08:02.381 --> 00:08:04.817
Was this the kind of group that
if you were shaky on the fife,

00:08:04.817 --> 00:08:05.885
they wouldn't have you?

00:08:05.885 --> 00:08:10.423
-If you could
kind of play the fife

00:08:10.423 --> 00:08:12.824
and you were amenable
to dressing up like

00:08:12.824 --> 00:08:19.098
a British grenadier,
they would have you.

00:08:19.098 --> 00:08:20.833
-That's quite a good
advertisement for membership.

00:08:20.833 --> 00:08:22.768
-It was. It was fun.
-Yeah.

00:08:22.768 --> 00:08:24.970
It's a cool thing to do.
-No, it wasn't.

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:26.304
Don't say that.

00:08:26.304 --> 00:08:31.143
I mean, everyone out there is
going, "Cool thing to do?

00:08:31.143 --> 00:08:34.146
That kid must have been
a winner in high school."

00:08:34.146 --> 00:08:35.648
[ Laughter ]

00:08:35.648 --> 00:08:37.382
I enjoyed it.
-It worked out alright.

00:08:37.382 --> 00:08:39.084
It worked out for you alright.
-It did.

00:08:39.084 --> 00:08:41.053
And I can still play the fife.

00:08:41.053 --> 00:08:42.154
-Can you really?
-Yes.

00:08:42.154 --> 00:08:43.422
-Wow!
Have you had any reason to?

00:08:43.422 --> 00:08:46.125
-"Wow!" That was the most
insincere "Wow."

00:08:46.125 --> 00:08:47.926
-Yeah. "Wow!"
-"Wow!"

00:08:47.926 --> 00:08:49.294
-Oh, my God! What's that?

00:08:49.294 --> 00:08:53.131
Oh, no!
I got to go to commercial.

00:08:53.131 --> 00:08:54.500
I'd love to hear
more about this fife.

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:57.403
-Fife? Wee!

00:08:57.403 --> 00:08:59.938
-You guys, that's Steve Carell.
-I wish we had a fife!

00:08:59.938 --> 00:09:01.640
-I wish we had a fife.

00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.143
"Minions: The Rise of Gru"
is in theaters July 1st.

00:09:04.143 --> 00:09:06.345
We'll be right back with
Machine Gun Kelly. Thank you.

